            0.085
          ],
          "content": "58"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.184,
            0.068,
            0.488,
            0.087
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Manjari | Grade 9 | Part I"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.107,
            0.886,
            0.26
          ],
          "content": "Why do some rational numbers have repeating decimal representations? Imagine calculating $\\frac { 1 } { 7 }$ using long division. You are dividing by 7. What are the possible remainders at each step? They can only be 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, or 6 (why not 0?). Because there is a limited number of possible remainders eventually, a remainder must show up a second time. Once a remainder repeats, the entire division process loops, creating a repeating decimal!"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.281,
            0.649,
            0.304
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Predicting the Type of Decimal Expansion"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.31,
            0.885,
            0.37
          ],
          "content": "The interesting thing is that we can predict whether the decimal expansion of a rational number will terminate or go on forever without actually doing the long division process!"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.369,
            0.882,
            0.389
          ],
          "content": "For this, we resort to finding the **prime factors** of the denominator."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.393,
            0.885,
            0.446
          ],
          "content": "Let $\\frac { p } { q } ,   q \\neq 0$ , be a rational number in its lowest terms (that is, p and q have no common factor other than 1)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.451,
            0.83,
            0.483
          ],
          "content": "Consider the fraction $\\frac { 3 } { 2 0 }$ . The prime factorisation of 20 is $2 ^ { 2 } \\times 5$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.499,
            0.601,
            0.544
          ],
          "content": "Hence, $\\frac{3}{20} = \\frac{3}{2^{2} \\times 5} = \\frac{3 \\times 5}{2^{2} \\times 5 \\times 5} = \\frac{15}{100} = 0.15$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.557,
            0.884,
            0.598
          ],
          "content": "We multiply the numerator and denominator by 5 to get 100 and arrive at 0.15 which is a terminating decimal."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.616,
            0.399,
            0.64
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Think and Reflect"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.66,
            0.855,
            0.711
          ],
          "content": "The decimal expansion of $\\frac { p } { q }$ will be terminating precisely when the prime factors of q are only 2, only 5 or both 2 and 5. Can you explain why?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.727,
            0.885,
            0.786
          ],
          "content": "This is because in such cases, we can make the denominator a power of 10 by multiplying both numerator and denominator by a suitable number."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.788,
            0.687,
            0.823
          ],
          "content": "Converting Rational Decimals into the Form $\\frac { p } { q }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.829,
            0.494,
            0.849
          ],
          "content": "**Case 1: Terminating decimals**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.849,
            0.885,
            0.904
          ],
          "content": "We have already seen in earlier grades how to convert terminating decimals into the form $\\frac { p } { q }$"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
